# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mobile environment configuration - the LOCAL override.
#
# One variable controls where the app sends every request:
#
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL
#
# read in exactly one file, src/config/env.ts. No screen hardcodes a URL.
#
# You do not need this file to develop against the deployed backend: that is
# what `npx expo start` does out of the box, because the committed
# `.env.development` points at Railway. Use this file when you want the app
# to talk to a backend running on YOUR machine instead:
#
#     Windows PowerShell : Copy-Item .env.example .env.local
#     macOS / Linux      : cp .env.example .env.local
#
# then edit the value below and restart Metro with `npx expo start --clear`
# (env values are inlined into the bundle when Metro starts, and cached).
#
# Why `.env.local` and not `.env`: Expo reads env files in priority order
#     .env.development.local > .env.local > .env.development > .env
# so a plain `.env` would lose to the committed `.env.development`. `.env.local`
# wins, and it is git-ignored. `.env.production` is only read by production
# builds (`npx expo export`, EAS) - `expo start` never loads it.
#
# SECURITY - read before adding anything here.
#
# Expo inlines every EXPO_PUBLIC_-prefixed variable into the JavaScript bundle
# at build time. Anything in this file is readable by anyone who unpacks the
# app. It is public configuration, not configuration that merely happens to be
# stored on the client.
#
# NEVER put a secret here: no API keys, no database credentials, no tokens,
# no Django SECRET_KEY. Secrets belong in the root .env, which only the
# backend reads and which is never bundled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the Django REST API, including the version prefix and a trailing
# slash. The /api/v1/ suffix is part of the value: services ask for paths
# relative to it ('extraction/', 'compliance/'), so an origin on its own would
# send every request to the wrong place.
#
# A phone is not the computer running Django, so "localhost" does not work on
# a device. Pick the line that matches your setup:
#
#   Physical phone, backend on your laptop (use the laptop's LAN address):
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8000/api/v1/
#   Android emulator, backend on the same machine (10.0.2.2 is the host):
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8000/api/v1/
#   iOS simulator, backend on the same Mac:
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1/
#   Leave it EMPTY to let the app derive "the machine serving Metro, port
#   8000" - the laptop you ran `npx expo start` on, which is right for a
#   phone in Expo Go when Django runs on that laptop:
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=
#
# The backend must be started on all interfaces for a device to reach it,
#     python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000
# your LAN address must be in DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS in the root .env, and your
# firewall must allow inbound connections to port 8000.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8000/api/v1/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three targets, and which file selects each:
#
#   Local backend        this file, as mobile/.env.local        (git-ignored)
#   Railway, development mobile/.env.development                (committed)
#   Railway, production  mobile/.env.production                 (committed)
#
# A shell variable overrides every file, for a one-off run:
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL="https://other-host/api/v1/" npx expo start --clear
#
# Production traffic is HTTPS only: a release build on Android refuses
# cleartext (http://) connections, and iOS App Transport Security does the same.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
